INT. MUSEUM OF LOCKS AND KEYS - GALLERY OF CURIOSITIES - NIGHT

Glass cases glow under museum spots. Padlocks shaped like hearts, a chastity belt, a Houdini handcuff, a Chubb detector from 1818. The marble floor is clean enough to eat from.

CLEO ANTONOPOULOS, 30s, in a cleaner's grey uniform, pushes a mop bucket along the aisle. Slow. Humming. Wringing the mop, slopping water. At the far end of the gallery a vault door waits, painted gold. Fake. Almost.

BARNABY SOUTHWORTH, 50s, the curator, in a cardigan and bow tie, sits at the security desk with a crossword. He is happy. The room is quiet. A radio plays softly.

BARNABY
Seven letters. "Opener of doors."

CLEO
Key?

BARNABY
Too short.

CLEO
Skeleton?

BARNABY
Nine.

CLEO
Master.

BARNABY
Six.

Cleo smiles. Wrings the mop. Water pools near a floor drain by the case of antique keys.

BARNABY (CONT'D)
You're very diligent, Cleo.

CLEO
It's a good floor.

BARNABY
Most of the staff would've gone home.

CLEO
Most of the staff aren't paid by the hour.

He laughs. She pushes the bucket another yard. The wheels squeak. He doesn't look up. She taps a button on the handle of the mop. A quiet click.

The security camera in the corner swivels, slow, until it faces the wall.

BARNABY
(still filling the crossword)
"Skeleton" doesn't fit, does it?

CLEO
No.

BARNABY
Well, I'm stuck.

CLEO
I'll help.

She sets down the mop. Walks to the golden vault door and, from her bucket, produces a stethoscope and a small brass pick. She presses her ear to the cold gold. Turns a tumbler. Another.

GUARD ENOCH BALOGUN, 40s, a thick neck and a creaking belt, rounds the corner with a flashlight, whistling. He stops. Looks at Cleo. Looks at the open bucket. Looks at the camera facing the wall.

ENOCH
Ma'am.

CLEO
(without turning)
Evening, Enoch.

ENOCH
That's not on the schedule.

CLEO
No.

Barnaby finally looks up, sees the guard, sees Cleo, sees the pick. His pencil stops.

BARNABY
Oh, dear.

Enoch's hand drifts toward his radio. Cleo's hand does not leave the vault. Tumbler clicks. A heavy thunk.

CLEO
Enoch. Put the radio down.

ENOCH
Ma'am --

CLEO
Please.

He considers. The radio comes down. He steps aside, and with a courtly gesture, motions toward the vault.

ENOCH
After you.

Cleo pulls. The door swings open on an empty velvet chamber with a single card: GONE FISHING. She stares.

BARNABY
(softly)
Seven letters. "Opener of doors."

ENOCH
Trickster.

BARNABY
Yes, that's it.
